PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Key problem facts: insects require dietary cholesterol specifically as ecdysone precursor (cholestanol cannot substitute); 250 mg/L chol supports full development; 2 mg/L or 0 mg/L chol insufficient; 250 mg/L cholestanol yields zero adult survival. Maternal provisioning deposits sterol reserve in eggs whose size tracks mother's diet. Cholestanol can act as bulk/sparing sterol. 

Round-0 split: majority favor C (Death, No eclosion) or H (No eclosion, Death), arguing maternal reserve exhausted before eclosion/adult viability and 2 mg/L + cholestanol remains sub-threshold. Minority favor F or D, positing sparing effect lets trace chol suffice once bulk cholestanol supplied, or reserve allows normal development. 

Unresolved tension: whether sparing rescues 2 mg/L chol when maternal reserve absent, and whether pure-cholestanol adults eclose then die or fail to eclose. No consensus yet; evidence from thresholds alone insufficient to distinguish without assuming sparing extent. Preserve for next round: C/H vs F/D disagreement and sparing assumption.}

